{"id":"wtanceuk","ord":"a0","title":"Viewing"}
{"id":"wikilinks","parent":"wtanceuk","ord":"a0","title":"[[Wikilinks]] in documents resolve to links — today they render as literal text","desc":"Filed 2026-08-12 on the human's word (separate item, not folded into daily-notes): their vault's notes lean on Obsidian-style [[target]] and [[target|label]], which olai's markdown pipeline currently renders as literal bracket text (visible in the human's daily-note screenshot). Scope: resolve wikilinks at render time in the sanitising pipeline (markdown/render.ts + rewrite.ts territory) — [[target|label]] shows label, [[target]] shows target; resolution order: a served document whose basename or path matches → /doc/<path>; a node id match → /n/<id>; unresolved stays visibly distinct (muted, title says unresolved — never silently a dead link, per HACKING error-surfacing). Inline-only contexts (node titles via title.ts) get the same treatment. Tests over the resolution table and the sanitiser (a wikilink must not open an injection seam). Depends on nothing in flight; touches markdown/ only. NOT DISPATCHED."}
{"id":"5kz73j46","ord":"a1","title":"Agents"}
{"id":"47ps3bzc","parent":"5kz73j46","ord":"a0","title":"Orchestrate from the chat, not a terminal #human"}
{"id":"oic-wake","parent":"47ps3bzc","ord":"a0","title":"Nothing can wake a chat agent — the babysitting regression","todo":true,"desc":"Nothing can wake a chat agent — the babysitting regression. ACP turns are human-initiated; no MCP notification wakes an idle session; the MCP blocking waits (wait_agentState / wait_outputSettled) hold a turn open into the host's timeout.\n\nANSWERED IN DESIGN 2026-08-15 (the human asked \"can kolu MCP do this?\" — yes, with the right subscriber): the subscriber does not have to be the agent. Olai's server is the ACP CLIENT — the party that starts turns — and kolu MCP exposes subscribable snapshot-bearing resources: `urgency` (who needs attention) and `terminals` (per-terminal agent state), per kolu-mcp/src/expose.ts. So the wake path is kolu → olai's own subscription → olai starts a chat turn saying which lane settled. That build is the olai-subscribes node; this node closes when it lands. Known sharp edge for the first probe: kolu's `activity` resource cannot honor the read contract (fresh-subscriber-starts-empty, recorded in expose.ts:80) — urgency/terminals are the cells to lean on. Until then, babysitting stays the terminal orchestrator's detached watchers."}
{"id":"oic-shell","parent":"47ps3bzc","ord":"a1","title":"\"No shell, so no gh\" — contradicted; one live probe decides","todo":true,"desc":"FILED as \"No shell, so no gh\" — and CONTRADICTED 2026-08-15 by the human and by the code: the claim counted only the MCP servers the session is handed (olai's ops, kolu's) and forgot the agent's own built-ins. The chat runs the Claude Code adapter in bypassPermissions mode (interpret.ts:43, the 2026-08-09 design), and nothing puts Bash in disallowedTools (only AskUserQuestion gets that, and olai avoids it via elicitation.form) — so the built-in shell, and gh with it, should run without asking. ONE LIVE PROBE settles it: in a real chat session, run `gh pr checks` and confirm the tool call executes rather than stalls on a permission nobody can answer. If it runs, this node closes as wrong and the chat orchestrator's gh story is already whole; the original desc's real observations that survive either way — fs_readFile being repo-scoped, TUI screens truncating long reviews — matter only if the probe fails."}
{"id":"c8ruks63","parent":"47ps3bzc","ord":"a2","title":"Org-style properties: facts stop living in desc strings #needs-brainstorm"}
{"id":"props-parity","parent":"c8ruks63","ord":"a0","title":"PR: set_prop + prop: search + the drawer — both faces in one change","todo":true,"desc":"The user-facing half, full parity in one PR per the ruling: set_prop (null removes; refuses every system key toward its own verb — status→the mark verbs, date→set_date, after→set_after, created/changed→nothing, they are stamps); search learns prop:key and prop:key=value; the web gets the quiet drawer under the note (hidden when empty) with ••• menu add/edit/remove. Prototype to build against: https://claude.ai/code/artifact/48a09a59-5079-44fe-b99a-7f3a5fe49c90 (insertion order, not alphabetical). Open question to settle in this PR's design: whether search HITS carry props (one query = the lane board).","after":["props-floor"]}
{"id":"props-stamps","parent":"c8ruks63","ord":"a1","title":"PR: created + changed — the ops layer stamps, nothing else may","todo":true,"desc":"Workflowy's pair as the third kind of built-in: capture stamps `created`, every write op re-stamps `changed`; no verb exists for either and set_prop refuses both. Absent on migrated nodes — the ledger does not invent a past; git log is the archaeologist's tool. Display rides the drawer (props-parity), so this PR is ops-layer stamping plus the two rows appearing.","after":["props-floor"]}
{"id":"sbcbh1v3","ord":"a2","title":"Bugs"}
{"id":"socket-litter","parent":"sbcbh1v3","ord":"a0","title":"Servers never unlink their unix socket — 7,305 dead ones found","doing":true,"desc":"Found 2026-08-15 root-causing the bridge regression: 7,305 dead .sock files, servers never unlinking on exit. OBSOLETED BY DESIGN the same day: the human ruled the stdio MCP and the whole attach/unix-socket machinery out of existence (see stdio-retires) — no sockets minted means no litter. The dispatched lane got as far as red tests + fixes with the unit suite green before the ruling; its work dies with the machinery, salvage in the killed worktree's history if ever wanted. The 7,305 existing files are a one-line janitor sweep for the human: rm /run/user/1000/olai/*.sock (live servers re-create theirs)."}
{"id":"mcp-process-leak","parent":"sbcbh1v3","ord":"a1","title":"A disconnected olai mcp never exits — four found alive from three builds","doing":true,"desc":"Found 2026-08-15: four `olai mcp docs` processes alive from three builds after their sessions disconnected. OBSOLETED BY DESIGN the same day: the human ruled the stdio MCP subcommand out of existence (see stdio-retires) — no such process will exist to leak. The four live orphans are the human's one-line janitor kill. If the leak class resurfaces in some other subprocess, the stdin-end drain precedent is PR #94."}
{"id":"ei35v8gv","ord":"a3","title":"Regressions"}
{"id":"bridge-no-fallback","parent":"ei35v8gv","ord":"a0","title":"PR: an attached olai mcp survives its server — solo-boot fallback on peer death","todo":true,"desc":"OFFENDING PR: #175. The attached olai mcp inherited its server's lifetime with no fallback — three live ledger deaths in one hour proved it. SUPERSEDED BY DESIGN 2026-08-15, the same day it was filed: the human ruled the stdio MCP out of existence entirely (stdio-retires) — .mcp.json dials the running server over HTTP, so there is no attacher left to fall back. The design conversation that got here is worth keeping: two stores over one directory have no cross-process protection (whole-file last-writer-wins, per-brain validation that breaks on disk, two git committers), so ONE BRAIN is the law — enforced henceforth by the directory lock (brain-lock) rather than by attach machinery."}
{"id":"mcp-stdout-pollution","parent":"sbcbh1v3","ord":"a2","title":"olai mcp logs onto stdout — warnings corrupt the JSON-RPC pipe","desc":"Was: `olai mcp` logged warnings onto stdout, corrupting the JSON-RPC pipe. OBSOLETE 2026-08-15: PR #184 deleted the `olai mcp` subcommand entirely — there is no stdio pipe to corrupt. Agents dial the running server over HTTP, where logs and the protocol never share a stream."}
{"id":"now","ord":"a4","title":"Now","desc":"Ongoing work. Each entry is a MIRROR of the item being worked, so its mark, note and subtree reflect live — Workflowy-style. The tracking list (Kolu terminal, current #stage hashtag, PR link) lives on the item itself, where a mirror's four fields cannot."}
{"id":"htm-extension","parent":"wtanceuk","ord":"a1","title":"PR: .htm joins the registry — one line, the day somebody has one","todo":true,"desc":"Deferral from PR #189 (2026-08-15): kinds.ts:73 deliberately claims one spelling per kind, so .htm is unclaimed. The registry makes this exactly one entry (that being true was the point of the registry); a vault holding a real .htm is the trigger."}
{"id":"create-outline-root-nudge","parent":"sbcbh1v3","ord":"a3","title":"PR: create_outline refuses the served root's own name instead of minting docs/docs","todo":true,"desc":"Deferred twice from PR #182 with the shape argued (2026-08-15): create_outline's path is judged through creatable (plan.ts:1477) against a Scope (plan.ts:216) that does not know where the served root is or what it is called — so `docs/agents.jsonl` handed to a server whose root IS docs minted docs/docs silently (it happened; a git mv undid it). The fix threads the served directory into Scope and through ops.ts — its own change, not a rider. The refusal wants a sentence: a first path segment equaling the served root's own name is at least a nudge."}
{"id":"stray-file-green-ci","parent":"sbcbh1v3","ord":"a4","title":"PR: CI fails on an unexplained new tracked file — green stops being blind to strays","todo":true,"desc":"Hazard proven live on PR #178 (2026-08-15): a reviewer's scratch file named `60` at the repo root was swept in by `git add -A` and NINE required CI lanes passed over it THREE times — green CI says nothing about files no test imports. A person reading the tree caught it. The shape of the fix: a required check (or claims-sweep arm) that fails when a PR adds a tracked file no manifest/test/doc accounts for — an allowlist of expected top-level entries at minimum, the tests/extension.test.ts sweep style. The counter-argument to weigh in the PR: every legitimate new file pays a toll; the toll must be one obvious line, or the check will be resented and exempted to death."}
{"id":"website-quiet-face","parent":"wtanceuk","ord":"a2","title":"The website's illustration still draws the pre-quiet outline — redraw it to match #deferral","todo":true,"desc":"#210's one deferral: website/index.html's hand-written illustration still shows accent tag pills, an always-shown note line, and two gutter columns — the face #210 retired. Redraw it to the quiet face (dim inline tags, pilcrow, folded notes) once the design has settled in daily use; it is an illustration, not the app, so it deliberately followed the design pass rather than preceding it.","created":"2026-08-16T17:19:38-04:00"}
{"id":"7axjtuln","ord":"a5","title":"Editor"}
{"id":"md-rich-editing","parent":"7axjtuln","ord":"a0","title":"Rich-text markdown editing: write the document as it renders #needs-brainstorm","desc":"Filed 2026-08-13 on the human's word, immediately after #148 shipped plain-textarea editing. The next step up: editing the rendered document rather than its source — WYSIWYG-ish markdown (the class of ProseMirror/Milkdown/Tiptap, or olai's own take), where **bold** is bold while you type. Deliberately OUT of #148's scope by its brief. To brainstorm before dispatch: source-of-truth fidelity (round-tripping markdown without rewriting the user's spelling — the files-are-the-database constraint applies to formatting too), whether the plain textarea remains as an escape hatch, how the conflict/refusal story (#148's was-guard, Overwrite verb) carries over, and dependency weight vs olai's hand-rolled grammar.\n\nBRAINSTORMED AND ANSWERED 2026-08-18, with the human — the dispatch-ready ruling is `md-live-preview-editor` (see). Every question above got its answer there: fidelity is solved by construction (source stays the document model, Obsidian-style live preview; the ProseMirror/Milkdown/Tiptap class this node floated is ruled OUT for serializer round-trip churn), the was-guard and Overwrite carry over into autosave, and the dependency question was ruled the other way round — dependencies are not cost, handrolled code is, so the behavior comes from ink-mde (fallback ixora) and our code is glue. This node stays only as the paper trail of the question.","see":["md-live-preview-editor"],"changed":"2026-08-18T11:17:10-04:00"}
{"id":"colourful-palette","parent":"wtanceuk","ord":"a3","title":"A really colourful palette, nichely named","desc":"REJECTED by the human 2026-08-11 — work abandoned unmerged. Left unmarked on purpose: not done, and no longer anyone's task.\n\n- **The ask:** ONE new palette in theme/palettes.ts — saturated and vivid against the fifteen muted ones, 8+ visible hue families, niche one-word name, ink-on-paper AA per #69's precedent, picker-automatic.\n- **What happened:** two rounds never reached the ask — coruscant (near-monochrome purple), then prismatic (purple + orange two-tone). PR #107 closed, branch/worktree deleted, terminal killed. (Process had been: no review, orchestrator fields with screenshots at green CI.)\n- **What survives for a future attempt:** the 8-token system caps a pure palette at 8 hue families — each token must take its OWN family (the failed attempts tinted two); a docs page may need a palette-scoped mechanism beyond the tokens (syntax-highlight theme, heading ramp) to read as a spectrum.","see":["theming"]}
{"id":"whats-new","parent":"wtanceuk","ord":"a4","title":"What's new: the app tells you what changed, every PR, linking the PR","desc":"Filed 2026-08-19 from the human's ask, ruled in discussion. A \"What's new\" item in the APP HEADER with an unread dot (last-seen entry held via createPreference — the receptacle is there for exactly this); clicking opens the list of entries, newest first, each linking its PR (github.com/juspay/olai/pull/N).\n\nRULED: entries are HAND-WRITTEN, ONE PER PR — a data file every PR must touch, ENFORCED BY A CI CHECK (a PR that touches no entry fails, with an explicit skip marker for the rare PR that truly has nothing user-facing). The generated-from-merge-subjects option was considered and declined: the human wants editorial control (prose beyond the title, screenshots possible), accepting the per-PR tax. Entries render through the existing markdown pipeline — olai-native, no second renderer.\n\nBrief-time details: the entry file's format and home; the CI check joins the odu pipeline; orchestrator briefs must tell every author \"write your What's new entry\" once this ships.","created":"2026-08-19T13:36:53-04:00","changed":"2026-08-19T18:38:11-04:00","custom":{"from":"human 2026-08-19"}}
